&lt;p&gt;Gadi Eisenkot, the man who could unseat Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, cuts an understated figure. Almost always dressed in black, he speaks halting English and often answers interview questions with bullet-point lists, reflecting the military culture that shaped him.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For some voters ahead of Tuesday&amp;rsquo;s general election, the 66-year-old&amp;rsquo;s lack of charisma could be one of his biggest strengths.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Serious and unassuming, Eisenkot stands in stark contrast to Netanyahu, Israel&amp;rsquo;s longest-serving prime minister. Netanyahu is known for his polished, American-accented English and his ability to deliver headline-grabbing soundbites on US television and social media.&lt;/p&gt;
